  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 142 AM MST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nToday through Fri night...Nearly stationary upper level low off\nthe OR- CA coast has ejected another shortwave towards the \nPacfic Northwest. At this time, the shortwave is in western OR \nand timing its arrival in central and southern Idaho should be \nvery late afternoon or very early evening for the forecast area.\nThis wave does not have a good connection to subtropical \nmoisture and amounts should be about two thirds of what was \nreceived with the storm that is winding down at this time. On \nthe other hand, the air mass is colder and that may boost snow \ntotals. Overall, during the first 24 hour period of this storm, \nthe Sawtooth region (including Stanley Basin) and the Sun Valley\nregion appear to warrant Winter Weather Advisories for the \namount of snow. Snow is possible everywhere, but significant \naccumulations will be limited to areas above 5500ft elevation, \nand even most of those locations have light amounts in the first\n24 hours of start (tonight through Thursday afternoon). \nPrecipitation continues Thu night through Fri night as this \nfeature is also a slow moving storm.\n\nSunshine today will allow some strong warming compared to\nTuesday afternoon, as most highs yesterday were actually from late\nMonday night. Most ot the Snake River plain should cross the 40\ndeg F threshold. The increasing cloud cover tonight should keep\novernight lows seasonably mild, but still nearly all locations\nshould get below freezing. The mild start allows some warming\nfor Thu as the forecast area stays in the warm sector of the\nstorm. Cloud cover keeps temperatures seasonably mild for Thu\nnight, but the cold sector moves in for Fri and keeps\ntemperatures below 40 deg F for most.\n\nThe blustery wind will end during the morning hours, and is not\nexpected to repeat, although breezy conditions should develop\nagain in the southern hills and the eastern Magic Valley late\ntonight into Thu morning. Limited areas, mostly on ridges in the\nsouthern hills, could reach Wind Advisory levels. Beyond this\ntime and location, wind will get breezy at times, but not to\nsignificant speeds. \n\nSat through Tue...Sat appears to be a time between shortwave\ntroughs once again, like today, but some clusters appear to not\nhave much of an upper level ridge. By Sun, all clusters have\nthat OR-CA upper level low finally become an open wave and move\ninland. Timing and strength variability among the clusters\nindicate precipitation likely, but amounts and temperatures make\nsnow amounts difficult to predict out this far. Consensus breaks\ndown completely by Mon, so expect the Mon-Tue forecast to vary\nwidely for right now. The majority of clusters place southern ID\nunder an upper level ridge, but a significant minority continues\nto have upper level troughing in the western states.",
